# Prove BOTH the quotient and the remainder of a PRIVATE division — revealing the pair, hiding a, b.
#
#   glass prove examples/prove/private_divmod.glass a=17 b=5   # -> (3, 2), ACCEPT
#   glass prove examples/prove/private_divmod.glass a=100 b=7  # -> (14, 2)
#
# A proof can now RETURN a tuple (or record), not just a scalar or a string: every component is
# bound as a public output wire (the multi-wire binder `build_claim_mw`, the same one string results
# use) and revealed. `divmod` returns `(a / b, a % b)`; a and b stay private witnesses, and the
# verifier learns only the quotient/remainder pair. The Third Witness independently confirms both
# components. (Components must be scalars — a tuple/record containing a string or a nested structure
# ABSTAINs, never a wrong proof.)
fn divmod(a: Int, b: Int) : (Int, Int) = (a / b, a % b)

divmod(a, b)
